%[text] %[text:anchor:T_E4471F05] # Different Plots
%[text] In this module we will cover the more advanced ways you can plot your data. 
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B6CF205B] ## Resources
%[text] The following site is an excellent resource for reviewing different plot types and for determining when and how you should use them:
%[text] - Story Telling with Data: [https://www.storytellingwithdata.com/chart-guide](https://www.storytellingwithdata.com/chart-guide)  \
%[text] Not sure whether you should use a bar chart or a box plot? Check out these articles
%[text] - [Bar charts and box plots](https://www.nature.com/articles/nmeth.2807.pdf?origin=ppub): this article in Neuron nicely summarizes when you should use a bar plot and when you should use a box plot.
%[text] - [Box Plots v Bar Plots](https://pagepiccinini.com/2016/02/23/boxplots-vs-barplots/): this author falls strongly in the box plot camp \
%[text] Interested in learning about Beeswarm charts? Read these articles:
%[text] - [https://www.datascienceblog.net/post/data-visualization/boxplot\_alternatives/](https://www.datascienceblog.net/post/data-visualization/boxplot_alternatives/)
%[text] - [https://towardsdatascience.com/better-data-visualization-using-beeswarm-chart-bb46a229c56b](https://towardsdatascience.com/better-data-visualization-using-beeswarm-chart-bb46a229c56b)  \
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C71E49FD] ### Load the Data
%[text] For this module, we will load ten years worth of Denver weather data. I have stored this data in a .CSV file (spreadsheet) in a remote file on an Amazon Server. To load this file, you simply need the URL. 
%[text] …Note, if you don't have or can't find the weather\_data folder, you can download a copy from here: [weather\_data](https://www.dropbox.com/s/m61ntgu3olrbjfo/weather_data.zip?dl=1) 
%[text] We can load remote spreadsheet simply by entering the URL of the file
clearvars
url="https://saldenest.s3-us-west-2.amazonaws.com/anat6205_resources/UNIT_1/weather_data_merged.csv" %[output:8883c987]
T =readtable(url) %[output:4b47e1ac]
%[text] - Note, a copy of this data is also stored in the UNIT 1/data folder. The file is called "weather\_data\_merged.mat" and you can load it simply by double-clicking on it.  \
mmSetFigPublication %[output:1b329538]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A8A4302F] ## Data Overview
%[text] When you analyze a large table of data, you should first plot all of the data and look for any obvious trends.  
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0370ABB4] ## Line plot
%[text] [TOP](internal:T_E4471F05) || [Data Overview](internal:H_A8A4302F) | [Line Plot](internal:H_0370ABB4) | [Histograms](internal:H_F3824883) | [Box Plots](internal:H_8BAA090F) | [Beeswarm](internal:H_BE89318C) | [Scatter](internal:H_A62621AD) | [Grouped Plots](internal:H_E2272ECF) | [Error bars](internal:H_E730B6D8)
%[text] Recall that you should use line plots when your data changes over time.
%[text] Here we have 10 years worth of temperature data. Let's plot the minimum temperatures in September over 10 years. 
figure; %[output:96170c1f]
plot(T.MinTemperatureF) %[output:96170c1f]

xlabel('days') %[output:96170c1f]
ylabel('Temp (˚F)') %[output:96170c1f]
%[text] - here we see daily and annual fluctuations but without any general trends one way or another.
%[text] - Note, we can't input T.MDT as the x-input because the days are not continuous (i.e. they represent only one month out of the year for ten years.) \
%%
%[text] **Do the maximum temperatures show a trend?**
%[text] To overlay the Maximum temperatures, we use the **hold** function. We can add a title and a legend to the plot using the **title** and **legend** functions respectively. 
hold on % keep the max temps %[output:9a0a3f6f]
plot(T.MaxTemperatureF) %[output:9a0a3f6f]
title ('Temps in September','2005-2014') %[output:9a0a3f6f]
legend('min', 'max') %[output:9a0a3f6f]
%[text] - The maximum temperatures appear as a different colored plot overlaid atop the max temps
%[text] - If you didn't include the **hold on** command, the minimum temperatures would simply overwrite the maximum temps (the blue bars would be gone)
%[text] - However, if you run this code block twice, you'll get another bar plot of minimum temps overlaid atop the current plot (and you won't really be able to tell).
%[text] - The function **figure** can be used reset everything for a fresh start.
%[text] - Note, we didn't use just one call to plot for these two lines, because we didn't have both x and y for both lines. We just had the y vectors.  \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F6266814] ### Line Plot with x input
%[text] With a vector input, the function **plot** automatically places each bar at an increment of 1. Often this is what you want and it sort of makes sense here, where every data point represents a day in September. But when you have multiple years of September,  it is kind of hard  to keep track of the year. 
%[text] To clarify things, lets create a vector from 1 to 300 and then divide that vector by 30. Now, all values below 1 are temps from the first year. Values between 1 and 2 are temps from the second year, and so on. 
%[text] We then input into **plot** all the vectors at once, as follows:
figure %[output:7b1beb17]

x = (1:300)' / 30; % set x to a fraction of a year

hp = plot(x,T.MinTemperatureF, x,T.MaxTemperatureF); %[output:7b1beb17]

xlabel('year') %[output:7b1beb17]
ylabel('Temp (˚F)') %[output:7b1beb17]
%[text] - Now we have something that's a little easier to read along the x-axis
%[text] - And we used just one function call to **plot** with no need for **hold** \
%%
%[text] We can even relabel XTickLabels to make it even easier to read, which we can access through the plot handle, *hp.* Notice that the x-tick labels are just a cell array of strings. Also notice that *hp* is an 2X1 array, where the first and second elements in *hp* are the handles to the maximum and minimum lines, respectively.
hp(1).Parent.XTickLabel = cellstr(num2str(([2004 2005:1:2014])')) %[output:0216a6b3] %[output:0cc0a882]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F3824883] ## Histograms
%[text] [TOP](internal:T_E4471F05) || [Data Overview](internal:H_A8A4302F) | [Line Plot](internal:H_0370ABB4) | [Histograms](internal:H_F3824883) | [Box Plots](internal:H_8BAA090F) | [Beeswarm](internal:H_BE89318C) | [Scatter](internal:H_A62621AD) | [Grouped Plots](internal:H_E2272ECF) | [Error bars](internal:H_E730B6D8)
%[text] A histogram can be used to explore the distribution of temperatures (regardless of the date). A histogram creates bins of data (e.g. bins of 5˚, like 80-85˚F) and displays the count per bin.
figure; %[output:3b339f1c]
x = T.MinTemperatureF;
histogram(x) %[output:3b339f1c]
xlabel('Temp (˚F)') %[output:3b339f1c]
ylabel('Count per bin') %[output:3b339f1c]
title('Distribution of September Temps over 10 years') %[output:3b339f1c]
%[text] - here you can see that in the month of September in Denver across 10 years, there were a lot of cool nights \
%%
mmSetFigPublication %[output:01e1a8e1] %[output:5342955c]
%%
boxchart(x) %[output:1011d1eb]
%%
%[text] We can overlay a second histogram of the maximum temperatures using the **hold** **on** command and another call to **histogram**
hold on %[output:2a3cb2a8]
histogram(T.MaxTemperatureF) %[output:2a3cb2a8]
legend("Min","Max") %[output:2a3cb2a8]
%[text] - notice that there are more bins in the minimum temp histogram. By default, the function **histogram** uses an algorithm to determine the number of bins needed.  \
%%
%[text] You can manually set the number of bins simply by including the number as a second input. Here we set the bins for both histograms to be 20:
figure; %[output:8be502c1]
histogram(T.MinTemperatureF,20) %[output:8be502c1]
hold on %[output:8be502c1]
histogram(T.MaxTemperatureF,20) %[output:8be502c1]
xlabel('Temp (˚F)') %[output:8be502c1]
ylabel('Frequency') %[output:8be502c1]
legend("Min","Max") %[output:8be502c1]
%[text] - Notice how the number of bins can dramatically change the appearance of the histogram
%[text] - It is important to use the right number of bins that properly displays the distribution of data \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8BAA090F] ## Box Plot
%[text] [TOP](internal:T_E4471F05) || [Data Overview](internal:H_A8A4302F) | [Line Plot](internal:H_0370ABB4) | [Histograms](internal:H_F3824883) | [Box Plots](internal:H_8BAA090F) | [Beeswarm](internal:H_BE89318C) | [Scatter](internal:H_A62621AD) | [Grouped Plots](internal:H_E2272ECF) | [Error bars](internal:H_E730B6D8)
%[text] A box-and-whisper plot plots the interquartile range of the data. These types of plots show the shape of the distribution, its central value, and its variability.
%[text] ![](text:image:301c)
%[text] In MATLAB, you use the **boxchart** function to create a boxplot. In the simplest function call, you get one plot per column of data:
figure %[output:4172cd1d]
y = T.MaxTemperatureF;
boxchart(y) %[output:4172cd1d]
%[text] - The box represents the 25th and 75th percentiles
%[text] - The line in the middle of the box is the 50th percentile (median)
%[text] - The whiskers extend to the non-outlier maximum and minimum values
%[text] - Any scatter points beyond the whiskers are outliers in the data. Outliers are defined as values that are 1.5 times the interquartile range away from either the upper or lower quartile - basically really far from most of the data. You can change this definition if you want
%[text] - When using box plots, you should always annotate in the figure legend what the whiskers and outliers indicate (since they can mean different things in different plots) \
%%
%[text] If we input  a matrix with two columns, we get two box plots. 
figure(Visible="on") %[output:36afe338]
y = [T.MinTemperatureF   T.MaxTemperatureF]; % create two-column matrix of data
boxchart(y,'Notch','on') % plot data as box plots %[output:36afe338]
xticklabels({'Min' 'Max'}) % change the tick label from a number to a label %[output:36afe338]
ylabel('Temperature (˚F)') % add a label to the y-axis %[output:36afe338]
ylim([32 100]) % set y-axis limits %[output:36afe338]
%[text] - here, we turn on the 'notch' display which creates a notch around the median. Notches that don't overlap indicate a difference of the data at the 5% significance level, which makes sense for these two datasets (night is colder than day) \
%[text] Note, this only works if there are an equal number of values in each vector that you want to plot (e.g. here there are the same number of minimum and maximum temps). To see how create multiple box plots in the same axes with unequal numbers of data points, refer to the [grouping variable](internal:H_2036574B) section
%%
%[text] #### Add Mean to the plot
figure(visible="on") %[output:9ae81ba3]
y = [T.MinTemperatureF T.MeanTemperatureF T.MaxTemperatureF]; % create three-column matrix of data
boxchart(y,'Notch','on') % plot data as box plots %[output:9ae81ba3]
xticklabels({'Min' 'Mean' 'Max'}) % add 'Mean' as a tick label %[output:9ae81ba3]
ylabel('Temperature (˚F)') % add a label to the y-axis %[output:9ae81ba3]
ylim([32 100]) % set y-axis limits %[output:9ae81ba3]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BE89318C] ## Swarm Chart
%[text] [TOP](internal:T_E4471F05) || [Data Overview](internal:H_A8A4302F) | [Line Plot](internal:H_0370ABB4) | [Histograms](internal:H_F3824883) | [Box Plots](internal:H_8BAA090F) | [Beeswarm](internal:H_BE89318C) | [Scatter](internal:H_A62621AD) | [Grouped Plots](internal:H_E2272ECF) | [Error bars](internal:H_E730B6D8)
%[text] While box plots nicely summarize the distribution of the data, they give little indication of the data density. One solution is to create a swarm chart: a technically 1-dimensional scatter plot that displaces (or jitters) the data along the orthogonal axis so there is little or no overlap between points. Swarm charts are very useful when you want to display a lot of data points at once. 
%[text] - [https://www.datascienceblog.net/post/data-visualization/boxplot\_alternatives/](https://www.datascienceblog.net/post/data-visualization/boxplot_alternatives/)
%[text] - [https://towardsdatascience.com/better-data-visualization-using-beeswarm-chart-bb46a229c56b](https://towardsdatascience.com/better-data-visualization-using-beeswarm-chart-bb46a229c56b)  \
%[text] The function **`swarmchart`** accepts two vectors: 1 vector that contains positional location for the data along the x-axis and the other vector that contains the data itself. In this example, we will set all of the Max Temperatures to have an x-position of 1 and all of the Minimum temperatures to an x-position of 2. The **`swarmchart`** function will jitter that location by a fractional amount to ensures minimal overlap along the x-axis
%[text] We first create a data vector, *`y`*, simply by stacking the maximum temperatures atop the minimum temperatures. Then we create a positional vector, *x*, that is the same size as *y* by stacking a series of 1's on top of a series of 2's. 
figure(visible="on"); %[output:77c15ede]
x = ones(height(T),1); % create a vector of ones
y = T.MaxTemperatureF; % get the max temperatures as a vector
swarmchart(x,y) %[output:77c15ede]
ylabel('Temperature (˚F)') % add a label to the y-axis %[output:77c15ede]
ylim([32 100]) % set y-axis limits %[output:77c15ede]
%%
figure(visible="on"); %[output:9f4b4200]
x = [ones(height(T),1); ones(height(T),1)+1]; % stack 1's atop of 2's
x = categorical(x, [1, 2], ["Min","Max"]); % typecast to categorical: Min=1, Max=2
y = [T.MinTemperatureF; T.MaxTemperatureF]; % stack min temps stacked on top of max temps into a column vector

swarmchart(x, y,... %[output:9f4b4200]
"XJitterWidth",0.5,... % reduce jitter displacement along the x-axis %[output:9f4b4200]
"MarkerFaceAlpha",0.25,... % marker face transparency %[output:9f4b4200]
"MarkerFaceColor",'b',... % marker face color %[output:9f4b4200]
"MarkerEdgeColor","b",... %[output:9f4b4200]
"MarkerEdgeAlpha",0.75) % swarm chart %[output:9f4b4200]
ylabel('Temp (˚F)') %[output:9f4b4200]
ylim([32 100]) % set y-axis limits %[output:9f4b4200]
%[text] - Notice here that the temperature is plotted along the **y-axis.**
%[text] - The position of each data point is semi-randomly jittered across the **x-axis** to minimize overlap
%[text] - The maximum temperatures appear to skew towards higher max temperatures (~85˚C)
%[text] - The minimum temperatures appear to be more symmetrically distributed around ~50˚C \
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_459C273B] #### Swarm Chart + Plot Box 
%[text] Swarm charts are often combined with box plots to get the advantages from both types of plots.
%[text] Here, we convert the *x* vector into a categorical array to more easily label the x-axis. As a bonus, the same inputs can then be used for **boxchart** (this is an example of a grouped box plot, which we discuss in more detail in the section below). For a more pleasing aesthetic, we add some additional inputs into **swarmchart**.
figure(Visible="on") %[output:8b5fea02]
hold on % hold can be turned on at any time %[output:8b5fea02]

x = [ones(height(T),1); ones(height(T),1)+1]; % stack 1's atop of 2's
x = categorical(x, [1, 2], ["Min","Max"]); % typecast to categorical: Min=1, Max=2
y = [T.MinTemperatureF; T.MaxTemperatureF]; % stack min temps stacked on top of max temps into a column vector

swarmchart(x,y,... %[output:8b5fea02]
"XJitterWidth",0.5,... % reduce jitter displacement along the x-axis %[output:8b5fea02]
"MarkerFaceAlpha",0.1,... % marker face transparency %[output:8b5fea02]
"MarkerFaceColor",'k',...% marker face color %[output:8b5fea02]
"MarkerEdgeColor","k",... %[output:8b5fea02]
"MarkerEdgeAlpha",0.15) %[output:8b5fea02]

hb=boxchart(x,y,'Notch',"on") % box plot %[output:6f520e85] %[output:8b5fea02]
ylabel('Temp (˚F)') %[output:8b5fea02]
set(gca,'FontSize',16) %[output:8b5fea02]
%%
%[text] Course function **mmBoxSwarm**
figure; %[output:66920293]
mmBoxSwarm(x,y,XJitterWidth=0.5,MarkerFaceColor='k',MarkerFaceAlpha=0.1,Notch='off') %[output:66920293]
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A62621AD] ## Scatter plots and correlation
%[text] [TOP](internal:T_E4471F05) || [Data Overview](internal:H_A8A4302F) | [Line Plot](internal:H_0370ABB4) | [Histograms](internal:H_F3824883) | [Box Plots](internal:H_8BAA090F) | [Beeswarm](internal:H_BE89318C) | [Scatter](internal:H_A62621AD) | [Grouped Plots](internal:H_E2272ECF) | [Error bars](internal:H_E730B6D8)
%[text] Swarm charts are basically 1-dimensional scatter plots, where you plot just one variable per swarm to visualize the distribution of the data in that vector. 
%[text] By comparison, **scatter plots** are two-dimensional plots most often used to compare two variables and see if the variables correlate in any fashion.
%[text] For example, here we compare the Minimum and Maximum temperature in a given day. 
%[text] In MATLAB, the function for scatter plots is simply called **scatter.** This function expects two vectors as the first two inputs. Here, we input the minimum and maximum temperatures. Since these vectors are pulled from the same table, the same row across the two vectors corresponds to the same day. Thus, here,  the x-axis indicates the day's minimum temperature, while the y-axis indicates the day's maximum temperature.
figure %[output:8209b742]
x = T.MinTemperatureF;
y = T.MaxTemperatureF;
hs = scatter(x, y); %[output:8209b742]
xlabel('Min Temp(˚F)') %[output:8209b742]
ylabel('Max Temp (˚F)') %[output:8209b742]
title('Minimum vs Maximum temps') %[output:8209b742]
%[text] - Each dot corresponds to a single day
%[text] - The fact that the temperatures trend upwards suggests the temperatures are correlated. This makes sense: warm days typically have warm nights
%[text] - The dots in this plot appear vertically aligned because the temperatures were rounded to the nearest degree. If you look carefully, they are also horizontally aligned. \
%%
%[text] We can change the marker face color through the scatter plot handle, *hs*
set(hs, 'MarkerFaceColor','cyan','MarkerFaceAlpha',0.5,'LineWidth',1) %[output:66bc1651]
%[text] - here we use the **set** function, which allows us to change multiple properties of the scatter plot using one function call \
%[text] 
%%
%[text] We can use the **corr** function to calculate the correlation between the two datasets. We use the functions **polyfit** and **polyval** to calculate a simple linear regression and overlay the regression line on the figure with the **plot** function.
hold on %[output:0850a7eb]

c = corr(x,y); % calculate a correlation between x and y
p = polyfit(x,y,1); % simple linear regression
f = polyval(p,x); % calculate Max temps based on min temps from line
hold on %[output:0850a7eb]
plot(x,f,'--k',"LineWidth",1) %[output:0850a7eb]
legend({'temperatures','linear fit'},"Location","best") %[output:0850a7eb]
title('Night and Day', sprintf('Correlation = %1.2f',c)) % add a subtitle by adding a second input to title %[output:0850a7eb]
%[text] - so, Minimum and Maximum temperatures appear to be positively correlated
%[text] - Notice that the regression line goes through the middle of the cloud
%[text] - The line and correlation roughly predict that warmer days have warmer nights (and vice versa) \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0BA81A90] ### Scatter Plots and Multiple Comparisons
%[text] In our table, we have 23 different variables, including Max and Min temperatures. To see if any of the other variables correlate with max (or min) temperatures, we need to plot each variable against temperature, one by one. Fortunately, we can use the power of a FOR LOOP and plot tiling to greatly simplify this process. We will also only use a subset of the Variables that we manually select.
figure %[output:009209df]
varnames = ["MaxDewPointF" "MaxSeaLevelPressureIn" "MaxWindSpeedMPH" "MaxHumidity" "MaxGustSpeedMPH" "CloudCover"];

y = T.MaxTemperatureF;
% y2 = T.MinTemperatureF;
for n=1:numel(varnames) %[output:group:893439bb]
    x = T.(varnames{n}); % programmatically access a Table Variable
    nexttile % tile %[output:009209df]
    scatter(x,y)

    hold on 

    p = polyfit(x,y,1); % calculate simple linear regression
    f = polyval(p,x); % calculate Max temps based on min temps from line

    plot(x,f,'--k',"LineWidth",1) % plot regression line

    title(varnames{n},sprintf('max corr: %1.2f',corr(x,y,'rows',"complete")))
    ylabel('Max Temp (˚F)')
end %[output:group:893439bb]
%[text] - The temperatures seem to be loosely negatively correlated to Humidity, cloud cover, and barometric pressure.
%[text] - Cloud cover appears to be correlated in a non-linear fashion
%[text] - Max Gust Speed does not have a regression line because it contains NaN values (Not-a-number) \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E2272ECF] ## Grouped Plots
%[text] [TOP](internal:T_E4471F05) || [Data Overview](internal:H_A8A4302F) | [Line Plot](internal:H_0370ABB4) | [Histograms](internal:H_F3824883) | [Box Plots](internal:H_8BAA090F) | [Beeswarm](internal:H_BE89318C) | [Scatter](internal:H_A62621AD) | [Grouped Plots](internal:H_E2272ECF) | [Error bars](internal:H_E730B6D8)
%[text] Grouped plots are used to plot subsets of the data. Using grouping variables, we can group the different rows of the table into different categories, isolating these subsets of data. Notice that in our table we have columns of numbers, such as temperature, dewpoint, humidity, and windspeed, and columns of identification, such as date (MDT) and event (e.g., rain, snow, etc.). The identifying data are also known as grouping variables and can be used to group rows into different categories.
%[text] These grouping variables are more useful when you typecast them to certain variable types, like **datetime** and **categorical**
T.MDT = datetime(T.MDT); % good for time data
T.Events = categorical(T.Events); % good for statistical organization
T.Events = addcats(T.Events,'none'); % add a category of none
T.Events(isundefined(T.Events)) = 'none'; % set all undefined events to the category of none
categories(T.Events) % list the categories found in events %[output:903f23bc]
%[text] - datetime is used to organize times and dates
%[text] - categorical is used to bin identifiers into categories. For example, there are several categories in the Events column such as Fog or Rain. There is also an undefined category \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_2036574B] ### Grouped box chart
%[text] Here we plot the max temperature data grouped by event
figure %[output:7bf5484b]
boxchart(T.Events,T.MaxTemperatureF) % first input is a grouping variable %[output:7bf5484b]
title('Max Temp by Category') %[output:7bf5484b]
ylabel('Temp (˚F)') %[output:7bf5484b]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C6F658D4] ### Grouped Box plot with swarm chart
%[text] Here is an example on how to overlay a jitter scatter plot on a box plot. Since the box plots were plotted by category, we need to set the x-values in scatter plots to the same category. We do this one category (and one box plot) at a time. For example, the first scatter plot are the temperatures that occured during a fog. 
hold on % overlay on box plots %[output:5edfc188]
cats = categories(T.Events); % cell array of all of the categories in events
cat_count = countcats(T.Events); % count of each category
for n=1:numel(cats) %[output:group:5104e8af]
    x = repmat(categorical(cats(n)), cat_count(n), 1); % create a categorical vector of one of the categories
    y = T.MaxTemperatureF(T.Events == cats{n}); % pull rows from Max Temp column that have the same category

    swarmchart(x,y,... %[output:5edfc188]
        "XJitterWidth",0.25,... % maximum jitter displacement along the x-axis %[output:5edfc188]
        "MarkerFaceAlpha",0.15,... % marker face transparency %[output:5edfc188]
        "MarkerFaceColor",'k',... % marker face color %[output:5edfc188]
        "MarkerEdgeColor","k",... %[output:5edfc188]
        "MarkerEdgeAlpha",0.25) %[output:5edfc188]
end %[output:group:5104e8af]
%[text] - The x-values in the scatter plot are categorical values
%[text] - Most data falls in the 'none' category \
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F78FED15] ### Grouped Scatter Plot
%[text] The statistical toolbox includes the function **gscatter** which allows you to color code your scatter plots based on group
figure %[output:345ea962]

x = T.MinTemperatureF;
y = T.MaxTemperatureF;
grp = T.Events; % grouping variable

gscatter(x, y, grp) %[output:345ea962]

xlabel('Min Temp (˚F)') %[output:345ea962]
ylabel('Max Temp (˚F)') %[output:345ea962]
title('Min vs Max temp by category') %[output:345ea962]
%[text] - Now we get different colored dots for the different categories \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E730B6D8] ## Error bars
%[text] [TOP](internal:T_E4471F05) || [Data Overview](internal:H_A8A4302F) | [Line Plot](internal:H_0370ABB4) | [Histograms](internal:H_F3824883) | [Box Plots](internal:H_8BAA090F) | [Beeswarm](internal:H_BE89318C) | [Scatter](internal:H_A62621AD) | [Grouped Plots](internal:H_E2272ECF) | [Error bars](internal:H_E730B6D8)
%[text] Error bars are used to indicate the variability in a bar plot. You should ALWAYS include an error bar if you are plotting means. That way, you can visually compare the variation between the means. The **errorbar** function is a separate function from the **bar** function. First you plot the bar plot then you overlay the error bars. Also, you should only plot data as bar plots if your data distribution is normal. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E482E481] ### Calculate Stats
%[text] For this example, we first need to calculate the stats. We will use **year** as a grouping variable, which we will pull  out of  the MDT column and add to a new column, called **Year**. 
dv = datevec(T.MDT); % break date down into components
T.Year = dv(:,1);
stats = grpstats(T,"Year",["mean","std"],"DataVars",["MinTemperatureF","MaxTemperatureF"]) %[output:263e6e78]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3A8CC400] ### Add Error bars to a Bar plot
%[text] Here we plot the average temp in a year as a bar plot and then overlay the error bars atop the bar plots (notice the **hold on** command). 
figure %[output:27a86934]

x = stats.Year;
y = stats.mean_MaxTemperatureF;
err = stats.std_MaxTemperatureF;

bar(x, y,"FaceAlpha",0.5) %[output:27a86934]
hold on %[output:27a86934]
errorbar(x,y,err,'k','LineWidth',1) %[output:27a86934]

ylabel('Temp (˚F)') %[output:27a86934]
title('Average Maximum Temps in September', '2005-2014') %[output:27a86934]
%[text] The important thing to remember for the **errorbar** function is that you need at least three (and sometimes four inputs). The first two inputs are identical to the inputs for the **bar** function, basically X and Y, or in this case, Year and Mean Temp. This sets the X and Y position of the center of the error bar. The third input is the extent of the error bar, above and below the mean. Here we plot that extent as standard deviation. If we want to separate extents above and below the mean, then we would have a third AND fourth input, where the third input would be the extent below the mean and the fourth input would be the extent above the mean. If you want no extent in one of these directions, enter an empty square bracket pair (\[\]) at the corresponding input. 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7D1B1817] ### Adding Error bars to a grouped bar plot
%[text] Recall, to group errorbars, you input a matrix into the y input. 
%[text] When the bar plots are grouped, the x-position of bars are offset. In this example, they are no longer exactly at the Year tick (they are slightly before or after that year tick). To properly add the errorbars in this situation, you need to find the center of these offset bars, which is stored in the "XEndPoints" field of the bar plot handle (bh).
figure %[output:671bb833]
y_mat = [stats.mean_MaxTemperatureF stats.mean_MinTemperatureF];

bh = bar(x, y_mat,'FaceAlpha',0.75); %[output:671bb833]

% overlay error bars
hold on %[output:671bb833]

xleft = bh(1).XEndPoints; % left bar positions (blue)
xright = bh(2).XEndPoints; % right bar positions (red)

yleft = stats.mean_MaxTemperatureF;
yright = stats.mean_MinTemperatureF;

err_left = stats.std_MaxTemperatureF;
err_right = stats.std_MinTemperatureF;

errorbar(xleft,yleft, [], err_left,'k','LineStyle',"none")  %[output:671bb833]
errorbar(xright,yright, [], err_right,'k','LineStyle',"none") %[output:671bb833]

% label axes
ylabel('Temp (˚F)') %[output:671bb833]
xlabel ('Year') %[output:671bb833]
legend('Max', 'Min') %[output:671bb833]
title('Average Temps in September', '2005-2014') %[output:671bb833]
%[text] - Here we set the third input to **errorbar** to empty brackets and the 4th input to the standard deviation of the temperature \
%%
%[text] and... we're done...
